Key questions:
1. The Go types kzg.Bytes48 and kzg.Cell are FIXED-SIZE arrays. When you cast []byte → [48]byte (Bytes48) or []byte → [BytesPerCell]byte (Cell), what happens if the source slice is wrong length? In Go, you can't directly cast []byte to fixed-size array — you use explicit type cast like kzg.Bytes48(sidecar.KzgCommitments[i]). What is sidecar.KzgCommitments[i]'s type?
2. The SSZ-encoded DataColumnSidecar: are KzgCommitments, KzgProofs, Column fields fixed or variable size types in SSZ? If fixed size, SSZ deserialization would enforce length. If variable, the length check in VerifyDataColumnSidecar only checks COUNT equality (len(Column)==len(KzgCommitments)==len(KzgProofs)), NOT that each element is the correct byte length.
3. For VerifyCellKZGProofBatch: the function takes []Bytes48 for commitments/proofs and []Cell for cells — all fixed-size Go array types. No runtime length check is needed because Go's type system enforces it at the point of conversion. But if the upstream type for KzgCommitments[i] is []byte (variable-length), what happens when you cast it to [48]byte?
4. Array length consistency in VerifyDataColumnsSidecarKZGProofs: the loop appends one entry per sidecar.Column row, so all four slices (commitments, indices, cells, proofs) are always built in the same loop and will always have equal length. No mismatch possible here.

